#{[#edb/ref 17592186050748 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055832} :activity/id #uuid "9b5534e2-3cf4-5579-abb7-0097d0103230" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055832 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055988 :tutorial/content "If we are given one or more factors of a polynomial, we can use these factors to continue factoring the polynomial completely. We just need to divide the polynomial by the known factors, and then continue factoring the quotient.\n\nFor example, suppose we know that $(x+2)$ is a factor of $f(x) = x^4+4x^3+x^2-6x.$ Then $(x+2)$ divides $f(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x+2)$ using synthetic division:\n\n![](images/25/25fd1f5f9f11398a68dc10e4f3ff5ad5368fa3e23d9fa336cb57324290cc8247.svg)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^4+4x^3+x^2-6x}{x+2} = x^3+2x^2-3x,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nx^4+4x^3+x^2-6x = (x+2) (x^3+2x^2-3x).\n$$\n\nWe can factor the cubic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+2)({x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}-3x) & =(x+2)(x)({x}^{2}+2x-3) \\\\  & =(x+2)(x)(x+3)(x-1) \\\\  & =x(x+2)(x+3)(x-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055833}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055833 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054803 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054574 :question/id #uuid "ae9cba85-b0ed-59c9-bf9f-682b921c5ee4" :question/math-academy-id "e-6258" :question/problem "Given that $(x-1)$ and $(x-3)$ are factors of the polynomial $f(x)= x^4 - x^3 - 7 x^2 + x + 6,$ factor $f(x)$ completely." :question/worked-solution "To factor $f(x)$ completely, we need to find its other factors.\n\nBecause $(x-1)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ we know that $(x-1)$ divides $f(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x-1)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | $x^4$ | $x^3$ | $x^2$ | $x^1$ | $x^0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\color{red}1$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-7$ | $1$ | $6$ |\n|  | $\\color{gray}\\downarrow$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-7$ | $-6$ |\n|  | $1$ | $0$ | $-7$ | $-6$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^4 - x^3 - 7 x^2 + x + 6}{x-1} = x^3-7x-6,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nx^4 - x^3 - 7 x^2 + x + 6 = (x-1) (x^3-7x-6).\n$$\n\nNow, because $(x-3)$ is also a factor of $f(x),$ it must be a factor of the quotient $(x^3-7x-6).$ So, we divide this quotient by $(x-3)$ using synthetic division once more:\n\n|  | $x^3$ | $x^2$ | $x^1$ | $x^0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\color{red}3$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-7$ | $-6$ |\n|  | $\\color{gray}\\downarrow$ | $3$ | $9$ | $6$ |\n|  | $1$ | $3$ | $2$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^3-7x-6}{x-3} = x^2+3x +2,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nx^4 - x^3 - 7 x^2 + x + 6 =  (x-1) (x-3) (x^2+3x +2).\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\nx^4 - x^3 - 7 x^2 + x + 6 =  (x-1) (x-3) (x+2)(x+1).\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Quartic Polynomial Given Two Factors"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055834}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055834 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054804 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054575 :question/id #uuid "7e2e4745-f999-5b5e-bf4b-22b9596c384c" :question/math-academy-id "e-6259" :question/problem "Let $p(x) = x^4 - x^3 - 9 x^2 - 11 x - 4.$ Knowing that $p(4) = p(-1) = 0,$ factor the polynomial $p(x)$ completely." :question/worked-solution "Since $p(4) = p(-1) = 0,$ the factor theorem guarantees that $(x - 4)$ and $(x + 1)$ are both factors of $p(x).$\nTo factor $p(x)$ completely, we need to find its other factors.\n\nSince $(x - 4)$ is a factor of $p(x),$ we know that $(x - 4)$ divides $p(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $p(x),$ we can divide it by $(x - 4)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | $x^4$ | $x^3$ | $x^2$ | $x^1$ | $x^0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\color{red}4$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-9$ | $-11$ | $-4$ |\n|  | $\\color{gray}\\downarrow$ | $4$ | $12$ | $12$ | $4$ |\n|  | $1$ | $3$ | $3$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^4 - x^3 - 9 x^2 - 11 x - 4}{x-4} = x^3+3x^2+3x+1,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nx^4 - x^3 - 9 x^2 - 11 x - 4 = (x-4) (  x^3+3x^2+3x+1).\n$$\n\nNow, because $(x+1)$ is also a factor of $p(x),$ it must be a factor of the quotient $( x^3+3x^2+3x+1).$ So, we divide this quotient by $(x+1)$ using synthetic division once more:\n\n|  | $x^3$ | $x^2$ | $x^1$ | $x^0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\color{red}-1$ | $1$ | $3$ | $3$ | $1$ |\n|  | $\\color{gray}\\downarrow$ | $-1$ | $-2$ | $-1$ |\n|  | $1$ | $2$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{ x^3+3x^2+3x-1}{x+1} = x^2+2x+1,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nx^4 - x^3 - 9 x^2 - 11 x - 4 = (x-4)(x+1)(x^2+2x+1).\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{4}-{x}^{3}-9{x}^{2}-11x-4 & =(x-4)(x+1)(x+1)^{2} \\\\  & =(x-4)(x+1)^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Quartic Polynomial Given Two Roots"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055835}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055835 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054805 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054573 :question/id #uuid "99356744-762f-57de-8bc5-9da70ddefa90" :question/math-academy-id "e-6203" :question/problem "Let $f(x) = 3 x^4 - 5 x^3 - 3 x^2 + 6 x - 8.$ If $x=2$ and $x=-\\dfrac{4}{3}$ are zeros of $f(x),$ factor $f(x)$ completely." :question/worked-solution "Because $x=2$ and $x=-\\dfrac{4}{3}$ are both zeros of $f(x),$ the factor theorem guarantees that $(x-2)$ and $\\left( x + \\dfrac{4}{3} \\right)$ are both factors of $f(x).$ So, $(x-2)$ and $\\left( x + \\dfrac{4}{3} \\right)$ both divide $f(x).$\n\nTo begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by one of these factors (it doesn't matter which one we divide first). Let's divide $f(x)$ by $(x-2)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | $x^4$ | $x^3$ | $x^2$ | $x^1$ | $x^0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\color{red}2$ | $3$ | $-5$ | $-3$ | $6$ | $-8$ |\n|  | $\\color{gray}\\downarrow$ | $6$ | $2$ | $-2$ | $8$ |\n|  | $3$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $4$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =3{x}^{4}-5{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+6x-8 \\\\  & =(x-2)(3{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}-x+4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, because $\\left( x+\\dfrac{4}{3} \\right)$ is also a factor of $f(x),$ it must be a factor of the quotient $3x^3 + x^2 - x +4.$ So, we divide this quotient by $\\left( x+\\dfrac{4}{3} \\right)$ using synthetic division once more:\n\n|  | $x^3$ | $x^2$ | $x^1$ | $x^0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\color{red}-\\dfrac{4}{3}$ | $3$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $4$ |\n|  | $\\color{gray}\\downarrow$ | $-4$ | $4$ | $-4$ |\n|  | $3$ | $-3$ | $3$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}-x+4 & =(x+\\frac{4}{3})(3{x}^{2}-3x+3) \\\\  & =(x+\\frac{4}{3})(3)({x}^{2}-x+1) \\\\  & =(3x+4)({x}^{2}-x+1),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{4}-5{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+6x-8 & =(x-2)(3x+4)({x}^{2}-x+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe quadratic polynomial $x^2-x+1$ does not appear to factor over the real numbers. We can make sure of this by verifying that its discriminant is negative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D={b}^{2}-4ac=(-1)^{2}-4(1)(1)=-3<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full factorization of $f(x)$ over the real numbers is given by \n\n$$\nf(x) = (x-2)(3x+4)(x^2-x+1).\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Quartic Polynomial With Only Two Real Factors"}}] :activity/title "Factoring Quartic Polynomials Using the Factor Theorem" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186054865}] [#edb/ref 17592186050409 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074759} :activity/id #uuid "f6ae1858-5ee1-5d31-814d-c2d09d20e0b8" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074759 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186075026 :tutorial/content "The shortest distance between a point and a line is the length of the line segment that connects the point to the line and is perpendicular to the line.\n\nFor example, consider the point ![](images/e2/e2002597cf3d8b01e24e7b4d2c6daf6fc9afb0afb3c6140877abfc3bf1be02c4.svg) and the horizontal line ![](images/06/06444505f504ecd01a91d4846b46bf202caafa9d9f756958483262f69b5db14b.svg) shown below:\n\n![](images/8c/8cba6df956e809981ac4a3ae78a6f6843be0ca8561fbcadd9d9ba98f2a5325cc.png)\n\nTo find the distance between the point and the line, we construct a segment with its endpoints on the point and the line, and which is perpendicular to the line\n\n![](images/80/802b30a788850457206716430755da2d5e3c6b3a53633c95626e49ee668d2507.png)\n\nThe distance between the point and the line is the length of this segment, which we compute as the absolute value of the difference in ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-coordinates of the point and the line:\n\n![](images/11/11e3682c2b811da299d9c475bd0ba3684d0c7f68e5677d925744b37026c47b0c.svg)" :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074760}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074760 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073810 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073434 :question/id #uuid "2ce03c34-8e2c-5554-a129-1eaf05ad46fd" :question/math-academy-id "e-12548" :question/problem "Find the shortest distance between the point $(3,2)$ and the line $x=5.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the given situation.\n\n![](images/95/952be9b27e5350f755f0f534883f3c70516bb4e03b34e9448ab5d43950d4bad6.png)\n\nSince the line $x = 5$ is vertical, the shortest distance between this line and the point $(3,2)$ is given by the absolute value of the difference between $x$-coordinate of the point and $5.$ \n\nSo, the shortest distance is\n\n$$\nd = |3 - 5| = 2.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Distance Between a Point and a Horizontal or Vertical Line"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074761}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074761 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186075027 :tutorial/content "Consider the line $l$ with the equation $y=-x + 6$ and the point $Q(2,1)$ that does not lie on $l.$\n\n![](images/83/8353825890413432e74d30512219cb95edc76fe8c24e7a824d6893c745c48bd1.png)\n\nSuppose we wish to find the point $P$ that lies on $l$ that's closest to the point $(2,1).$ \n\n![](images/f8/f880cfde6bdfe07c6804609f9b052189632afcde4907207de5245298aea9ea6e.png)\n\nNow, here's the key idea: $P$ is the point that lies on $l$ *and* lies on the line *perpendicular* to $l$ that passes through $Q.$\n\nSo, to find $P,$ we proceed as follows:\n\n- First, we find the line perpendicular to $l$ that passes through $Q.$\n\n- Then, we find the intersection of the two lines.\n\nWe know that one endpoint of the segment is [math](2,1).[/math] To find the other endpoint, we find the equation of the line that is perpendicular to [math]y=-x+6[/math] and passes through the point [math](2,1),[/math] and then find where it intersects [math]y=-x+6.[/math]\n\nThe line $y=-x + 6$ has a slope of $-1,$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $1$ (the negative reciprocal). It also must pass through the point $(2,1).$ So, using point-slope form, we find that the equation of the perpendicular line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-1 & =1\\cdot (x-2) \\\\ y-1 & =x-2 \\\\ y & =x-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find where the lines $y=-x+6$ and $y=x-1$ intersect, we set the equations equal to each other and solve for $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-x+6 & =x-1 \\\\ 6+1 & =x+x \\\\ 7 & =2x \\\\ x & =\\frac{7}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\dfrac{7}{2}$ into either of the two equations gives $y=\\dfrac{5}{2}.$ So, the coordinates of $P$ are $\\left( \\dfrac{7}{2}, \\dfrac{5}{2} \\right).$" :tutorial/title "Finding the Point on a Line Closest to Another Point"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074767}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074762 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073811 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073435 :question/id #uuid "64a6782b-aa64-5027-8311-3498c19c39e1" :question/math-academy-id "e-12549" :question/problem "Find the shortest distance between the point $(1,3)$ and the line $y=\\dfrac{1}{2}x -5.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's draw a diagram describing our situation. \n\n![](images/5c/5ccfa3c27c6bb2759ffc37c73b3640583fa962ee79f47b21f02ac0c1d9164b00.png)\n\n \n\nFirst, we find the equation of the line that is perpendicular to $y=\\dfrac{1}{2}x - 5$ and passes through the point $(1,3).$ \n\nThe given line has a slope of $\\dfrac{1}{2},$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $-2.$\n\nUsing point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the perpendicular line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-3 & =-2(x-1) \\\\ y-3 & =-2x+2 \\\\ y & =-2x+5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now find the point where the two lines intersect. To do this, we solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=\\frac{1}{2}x-5 \\\\ y=-2x+5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by setting the two equations equal and solving for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}x-5 & =-2x+5 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}x+2x & =5+5 \\\\ \\frac{5}{2}x & =10 \\\\ x & =10\\cdot \\frac{2}{5} \\\\ x & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=4$ into either of the two equations gives $y=-3.$ So, our solution is $x=4$ and $y=-3.$ Consequently, the lines intersect at the point $\\left( 4,-3\\right).$\n\nFinally, we find the distance $d$ between the points $(1,3)$ and $\\left( 4, -3 \\right)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})^{2}+({y}_{2}-{y}_{1})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(4-1)}^{2}+{(-3-3)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9+36} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{45} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9}\\sqrt{5} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the shortest distance between the point $(1,3)$ and the line $y=\\dfrac{1}{2}x -5$ is $3 \\sqrt{5}.$"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Distance Between a Point and a Line"}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074767 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073815 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073436 :question/id #uuid "57fc631b-c251-55d2-a9d5-6b41fc4bb00c" :question/math-academy-id "e-12612" :question/problem "![](images/c4/c48fc163b3c9543dcfdd03b20882f0d2a19747672c6b1db2a42a683f0014fe25.png)\n\n \n\nFind the point on the line $y-x=4$ that is closest to the point $(3,3).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the equation of the given line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-x & =4 \\\\ y & =x+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the equation of the line that is perpendicular to $y =  x + 4$ and passes through the point $(3,3).$ \n\nThe given line has a slope of $1,$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $-1.$\n\nUsing point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the perpendicular line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-3 & =-1(x-3) \\\\ y-3 & =-x+3 \\\\ y & =-x+6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now find the point where the two lines intersect. To do this, we solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=x+4 \\\\ y=-x+6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by putting the two equations equal to one another and solving for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+4 & =-x+6 \\\\ x+x & =6-4 \\\\ 2x & =2 \\\\ x & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=1$ into either of the two equations gives $y=5.$ So, our solution is $x=1$ and $y=5.$ Consequently, the lines intersect at the point $(1,5).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the point on the line $y-x= 4$ that is closest to the point $(3,3)$ is  $(1,5).$"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Point on a Line That Is Closest to a Given Point"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074768}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074768 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186075028 :tutorial/content "Earlier, we found that the point $P\\left(\\dfrac72,\\dfrac52\\right)$ lies on the line $y=-x+6$ and is the closest point on this line to the point $Q(2,1).$\n\n![](images/11/11901390427d5cd79af514a589175044cafebf47becdae84045005f4564de090.png)\n\nTo calculate the shortest *distance* between $Q$ and the line, we compute the distance between $P$ and $Q$ using the distance formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})}^{2}+{({y}_{2}-{y}_{1})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{7}{2}-2)}^{2}+{(\\frac{5}{2}-1)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{4}+\\frac{9}{4}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the shortest distance between the line $y=-x+6$ and $Q(2,1)$ is $\\dfrac{ 3 }{ \\sqrt{2} } .$" :tutorial/title "The Shortest Distance Between a Point and a Line"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074762}}] :activity/title "The Shortest Distance Between a Point and a Line" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186073946}] [#edb/ref 17592186051084 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097783} :activity/id #uuid "00c1cd54-a7af-5240-860b-4432978fec0c" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097750 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097807 :tutorial/content "Let's now prove the chain rule in the following form:\n\n$$\n(f \\circ g)'(a) = f'(g(a)) \\cdot g'(a)\n$$\n\nLet $f$ and $g$ be functions such that\n\n- $g$ is differentiable at $a$,\n\n- $f$ is differentiable at $g(a)$.\n\nThen $f \\circ g$ is differentiable at $a$, and we compute its derivative as follows.\n\nWe start with the definition of the derivative:\n\n$$\n(f \\circ g)'(a) = \\lim_{x \\to a} \\frac{f(g(x)) - f(g(a))}{x - a}\n$$\n\nSince $g$ is differentiable at $a$, it is also continuous there. Hence $g(x) \\to g(a)$ as $x \\to a$. For $x$ sufficiently close to $a$, we have $g(x) \\ne g(a)$, so we may write\n\n$$\n\\frac{f(g(x)) - f(g(a))}{x - a} \n= \\frac{f(g(x)) - f(g(a))}{g(x) - g(a)} \\cdot \\frac{g(x) - g(a)}{x - a}.\n$$\n\nTaking the limit as $x \\to a$, and using the product rule for limits (which applies since both limits exist), we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f∘g)^{'}(a) & ={lim}_{x\\to a}(\\frac{f(g(x))-f(g(a))}{g(x)-g(a)}\\cdot \\frac{g(x)-g(a)}{x-a}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(g(x))-f(g(a))}{g(x)-g(a)}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to a}\\frac{g(x)-g(a)}{x-a} \\\\  & ={f}^{'}(g(a))\\cdot {g}^{'}(a)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required." :tutorial/title "Deriving the Chain Rule"}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097783 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097872 :tutorial/content "The **chain rule** for differentiation states that the derivative of a composite function $f(g(x))$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}f} {\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac {\\textrm{d}f} {\\textrm{d}g} \\cdot \\dfrac {\\textrm{d}g} {\\textrm{d}x}.\n$$\n\nThere is some intuition behind this rule: if we think of the derivatives as fractions, the ${\\textrm{d}g}$'s would appear to cancel out.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\cancel{\\text{d}g}}\\cdot \\frac{\\cancel{\\text{d}g}}{\\text{d}x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo see how the chain rule can be used to differentiate a function like \n\n$$\nf(x) = (1+2x)^3,\n$$\n\nfirst, notice that the function can be written as a composite function\n\n$$\nf(g) = g^3, \\qquad g(x) = 1+2x.\n$$\n\nWe can use the chain rule to compute the derivative of this composite function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}g}({g}^{3})\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(1+2x) \\\\  & =3{g}^{2}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =6{g}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $g(x) = 1+2x$ for $g,$ we obtain the final result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =6(1+2x)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** We'll provide a derivation of the chain rule at the end of the lesson." :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097784}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097784 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096841 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096400 :question/id #uuid "19bf8d06-cbcc-547b-b882-ac89cd39fd1a" :question/math-academy-id "e-181" :question/problem "Given that $f(x) = (3x^4+x^3)^5,$ calculate $\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}f} {\\textrm{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f(x)$ as a composite function:\n\n$$\nf = g^5, \\qquad g = 3x^4+x^3\n$$\n\nThe chain rule states that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}f} {\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac {\\textrm{d}f} {\\textrm{d}g} \\cdot \\dfrac {\\textrm{d}g} {\\textrm{d}x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}f} {\\textrm{d}g}  = 5g^4,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}g} {\\textrm{d}x} = 12x^3 + 3x^2.\n$$\n\nFinally, multiplying the two together and substituting for $g$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =5{g}^{4}\\cdot (12{x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =5(3{x}^{4}+{x}^{3}{)}^{4}(12{x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Polynomial Raised to a Power Using the Chain Rule"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097785}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097785 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096842 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096401 :question/id #uuid "8fcdb2c8-71ca-5bfa-b7fe-49f8f2c05bbb" :question/math-academy-id "e-182" :question/problem "Given that $y = \\sqrt{1-x^2},$ find $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}.$" :question/worked-solution "We use the chain rule in the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d u}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x}.\n$$\n\nHere, we've used $u$ instead of $g$ for the intermediate variable. \n\nLet $u=1-x^2.$ Then $y = \\sqrt u = u^{1/2}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d u} = \\dfrac{1}{2}u^{-1/2} = \\dfrac{1}{2\\sqrt u}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x}  = -2x.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\\cdot (-2x) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2x}{2\\sqrt{u}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Radical Function Using the Chain Rule"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097786}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097786 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097873 :tutorial/content "The chain rule can also be expressed using primes instead of Leibniz notation.\n\nThe chain rule, in prime notation, states that\n\n$$\n(f(g(x)))' = f'(g(x)) \\cdot g'(x).\n$$" :tutorial/title "Another Notation for the Chain Rule"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097787}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097787 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096843 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096402 :question/id #uuid "2c37ae73-233d-5e0d-87ff-05cacc6d078b" :question/math-academy-id "e-183" :question/problem "Given that $f(x) = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{1-3x}},$ calculate $f'(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $g(x)=1-3x$ and $f(g(x)) = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{g}} = g^{-1/2}.$ \n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(g(x)) & =({g}^{-1/2}{)}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{2}{g}^{-3/2}=-\\frac{1}{2{g}^{3/2}}, \\\\ {g}^{′}(x) & =(1-3x{)}^{′}=-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the chain rule, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={f}^{′}(g(x))\\cdot {g}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2{g}^{3/2}}\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2{g}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2(1-3x{)}^{3/2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Function Using the Chain Rule and Prime Notation"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097750}}] :activity/title "The Chain Rule for Differentiation" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186096888}] [#edb/ref 17592186049482 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176707} :activity/id #uuid "d467d5f5-884a-5851-a89e-3df724c8eae2" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176705 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175970 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175820 :question/id #uuid "616e03ca-b0fc-53f1-baf0-8034d7731891" :question/math-academy-id "e-8885" :question/problem "$$\n\\mathbf{f}(t,z) = \\big\\langle \\text{_____}, \\: 4\\sin{t}, \\: z \\big\\rangle\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $\\mathbf{f}(t,z)$ above parametrizes the elliptic cylinder defined in Cartesian coordinates as $\\dfrac{x^2}{4}+\\dfrac{y^2}{16}=1.$ What is the first component of $\\mathbf{f}?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the ellipse $\\dfrac{x^2}{4}+\\dfrac{y^2}{16}=1$ in $xy$-plane has the parametrization\n\n$$\n\\langle x,y \\rangle = \\big\\langle 2\\cos{t}, \\: 4\\sin{t} \\big\\rangle,\n\\qquad t \\in [0,2\\pi),\n$$\n\nsince \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16} & =\\frac{(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}}{16} \\\\  & =\\frac{4{cos}^{2}⁡t}{4}+\\frac{16{sin}^{2}⁡t}{16} \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t,z) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t \\in [0,2\\pi)$ and $z \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$\n\nTherefore, the first component of $\\mathbf{f}$ is ${\\color{blue}2\\cos{t}}.$"} :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing an Elliptic Cylinder"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176891}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176707 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176984 :tutorial/content "In this lesson, we will explore strategies for parametrizing cylinders.\n\nParametrizing a cylinder is no more difficult than parametrizing its base curve. The only difference is that we need a *second* parameter to control the variation parallel to the cylinder's axis. This second parameter is usually allowed to vary arbitrarily.\n\nFor example, consider the parabolic cylinder defined in Cartesian coordinates as\n\n$$y = 8x^2.$$\n\nNotice that $y = y(x)$ is an explicit function of $x.$ Therefore, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on this surface, we have\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}f(x,z) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\ & =⟨x,\\,8{x}^{2},\\,z⟩.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nAnd that's it! We have successfully found a parametrization of this cylinder.\n\nNote that, under this parametrization\n\n- the parameter $x \\in (-\\infty,\\infty)$ controls the movement along the base curve, and\n- the parameter $z \\in (-\\infty,\\infty)$ controls the movement parallel to the $z$-axis.\n\nLet's see some more examples." :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176708}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176708 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175972 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175822 :question/id #uuid "3e65aff0-a768-58fd-b41c-ca38ce3eb0bd" :question/math-academy-id "e-8888" :question/problem "$$\n\\mathbf{f}(x,y) = \\big\\langle x, \\: y, \\: \\text{___} \\big\\rangle\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $\\mathbf{f}(x,y)$ above parametrizes the parabolic cylinder defined in Cartesian coordinates as $2x^2=z.$ What is the third component of $\\mathbf{f}?$" :question/worked-solution "For any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨x,\\,y,\\,2{x}^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $x \\in (-\\infty,\\infty)$ and $y \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$\n\nTherefore, the third component of $\\mathbf{f}$ is ${\\color{blue} 2x^2}.$"} :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing a Parabolic Cylinder"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176890}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176709 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175973 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175823 :question/id #uuid "3c0c59e2-ceaa-54ab-bb56-3cb6daadb5f3" :question/math-academy-id "e-8889" :question/problem "$$\n\\mathbf{f}(t,y) = \\big \\langle 4\\tan{t}, \\, y, \\, \\text{_____} \\big\\rangle, \\qquad t\\in\\left(-\\dfrac\\pi2,\\dfrac\\pi2\\right)\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $\\mathbf{f}(t,y)$ above parametrizes one sheet of the hyperbolic cylinder defined in Cartesian coordinates as $\\dfrac{z^2}{4} - \\dfrac{x^2}{16} = 1$ for $z \\geq 2.$ What is the third component of $\\mathbf{f}?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the hyperbola $\\dfrac{z^2}{4} - \\dfrac{x^2}{16} = 1$ for $z \\geq 2$ in $xz$-plane has the parametrization\n\n$$\n\\langle x , z \\rangle = \\big \\langle 4\\tan{t}, \\, 2\\sec{t} \\big\\rangle,\n\n\\qquad t \\in \\left(-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2},\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right)\n$$\n\nsince \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16} & =\\frac{(2\\operatorname{sec}⁡t)^{2}}{4}-\\frac{(4\\operatorname{tan}⁡t)^{2}}{16} \\\\  & =\\frac{4{sec}^{2}⁡t}{4}-\\frac{16{tan}^{2}⁡t}{16} \\\\  & ={sec}^{2}⁡t-{tan}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t,y) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y,\\,2\\operatorname{sec}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t \\in \\left(-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}, \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right)$ and $y \\in (-\\infty, \\infty).$\n\nTherefore, the third component of $\\mathbf{f}$ is ${\\color{blue}2\\sec{t}}.$"} :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing a Hyperbolic Cylinder"}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176890 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186177077 :tutorial/content "Let's find a parametrization of the elliptic cylinder defined in Cartesian coordinates as\n\n$$\\dfrac{x^2}{25}+\\dfrac{z^2}{16}=1.$$\n\nThe base curve in the $xz$-plane is an ellipse centered at $O$ with semi-axes of lengths $\\sqrt{25} = 5$ and $\\sqrt{16} = 4.$ Therefore, we can parametrize the base curve in the $xz$-plane as follows:\n\n$$\\langle x,z \\rangle = \\big\\langle 5\\cos{t}, \\: 4\\sin{t} \\big\\rangle, \\qquad t \\in [0,2\\pi).$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following parametrization:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}f(t,y) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\ & =⟨5\\operatorname{cos}t,\\,y,\\,4\\operatorname{sin}t⟩\\end{aligned}$$\n\nwhere $t \\in [0,2\\pi)$ and $y \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$\n\nNote that the axis of the cylinder coincides with the $y$-axis. Therefore, the parameter $y$ is allowed to vary arbitrarily." :tutorial/title "Elliptic Cylinders"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176705}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176891 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186177078 :tutorial/content "To find a parametrization of one sheet of the hyperbolic cylinder defined in Cartesian coordinates as\n\n$$\\dfrac{x^2}{25}-\\dfrac{y^2}{16}=1$$\n\nfor $x \\geq 5,$ recall that the corresponding hyperbola in $xy$-plane has the parametrization\n\n$$\\langle x,y \\rangle = \\big\\langle 5 \\sec{t}, \\: 4 \\tan{t} \\big\\rangle, \\qquad t\\in\\left(-\\dfrac\\pi2,\\dfrac\\pi2\\right)$$\n\nWe can check that this indeed parametrizes our base hyperbola by substituting the parametric equations into the equation of the base curve:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16} & =\\frac{(5\\operatorname{sec}t)^{2}}{25}-\\frac{(4\\operatorname{tan}t)^{2}}{16} \\\\ & ={sec}^{2}t-{tan}^{2}t \\\\ & =1\\end{aligned}$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}f(t,z) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\ & =⟨5\\operatorname{sec}t,\\,4\\operatorname{tan}t,\\,z⟩\\end{aligned}$$\n\nwhere $t\\in\\left(-\\dfrac\\pi2,\\dfrac\\pi2\\right)$ and $z \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$" :tutorial/title "Hyperbolic Cylinders"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176709}}] :activity/title "Parametrizations of Cylinders" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186176139}]}
